  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 325 PM MDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nMostly benign conditions to start the work week in the long\nterm, but this pattern should begin to be broken up by a deep\nPacific system that will encroach our region by the end of the\nforecast period. Monday starts with high pressure ridging mostly\nholding control of our forecast area, but a weakening upper\nlevel low will move across the Four Corners region and then just\nto our southeast through Tuesday afternoon. While mostly non-\nimpactful, this feature will at least bring a very low end\nchance of some high elevation precipitation, along with a small\nbump in moisture. This influx of moisture will continue through\nthe rest of the week as our upper level flow turns southwesterly\nwhile we sit between high pressure controlling the southeastern\nUS and a deep Pacific system that will move inland by later \nWednesday into Thursday. This feature will be the next \nnoteworthy system to impact the forecast area as we see \nprecipitation begin overspreading late Thursday into Friday and \nstart another cooling trend that should persist into the \nweekend. For this forecast period, expect mostly similar \ntemperatures day to day as highs will be around 6 to 12 degrees \nabove normal, generally in the 60's to 70's, with lows similarly\nwarm in the 40's. Any drop in temperatures would occur just \nbeyond the long term after the Pacific system moves in, and the \nmost impactful precipitation chances similarly just beyond this \nforecast time as well. Overall it should be a quiet period, but \nthat will be ending by the weekend after next.",
